<PageLayout
	title="About Me"
	eyebrow="Who's behind this?"
	description="Computer Engineering student at Palomar College, transferring to UC San Diego. Embedded systems, Meshtastic, self-hosting, and a lot of soldering."
>
	<p>
		I'm <strong>James Ankney</strong>, a Computer Engineering student at Palomar College, currently
		working on transferring to UC San Diego. This site is my workbench
		notebook: parts lists, wiring diagrams, firmware notes, and an honest
		record of everything that went right as well as everything that exploded
		along the way.
	</p>

	<p>
		It all started back in 2022 with a <a href="/projects/facial-recognition-soup-laser/">laser turret built out of a soup can, a webcam, and two servos</a>. It was a <a href="https://www.youtube.com/@michaelreeves">Michael Reeves</a>-grade bad idea and it mostly worked, and I've been hooked on building things ever since. These days most of what I make lives somewhere between hardware and software: ESP32 and nRF52840 boards, custom Bluetooth and LoRa firmware, a couple of Meshtastic nodes (one of which rides around in my car), and rebuilding cheap consumer electronics from scratch once the original board inevitably dies.
	</p>

	<p>
		The other half is software. I run Arch Linux and I've spent numerous hours pulling my digital life onto hardware I actually control: <a href="/projects/selfhosted-calendar/">self-hosting my calendar</a>, my media, <a href="/projects/downloading-wikipedia/">an offline copy of all of Wikipedia</a>, and eventually the VPN gluing it all together. Lately I've been deep in local AI too: <a href="/projects/local-llm-hosting-laptop/">running a 35B model on a laptop GPU that has no business doing it</a>, <a href="/projects/glados-voice-generation/">cloning voices</a>, and a <a href="/projects/local-llm-safety-tuning/">safety fine-tuning project</a> I'm still chipping away at.
	</p>

	<p>
		A few things I care about: building projects that actually get used instead of shelved, documenting the mistakes as thoroughly as the wins (the Pi-hole writeup is a project I undid, and that's on here on purpose), and picking parts I can actually source again when something breaks. I'm also slowly getting better at not burning out MOSFETs.
	</p>

	<p>
		If you want to see what I'm currently working on, check out the
		<a href="/projects/">project log</a>.
	</p>
</PageLayout>
